{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downtrend bias: when the recent 30d history is falling, the model remains too high (systematic positive bias). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path; increase the shift with horizon (small at days 1–5, larger by days 15–30).",
    "Horizon drift/over-optimism: errors grow with lead time; the model underestimates the magnitude of sustained selloffs. Add a negative slope correction (progressively lower forecasts as horizon extends).",
    "Insufficient mean-reversion to the downside: after a break lower, the model predicts stabilization/rebound too early. Delay any rebound signal; require multiple consecutive up-days in history before allowing forecasted recovery.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: when the model does project a bounce, it overshoots (forecast peaks too high vs realized). Cap rebound amplitude relative to the last observed price (e.g., limit upside to a modest %/€ amount unless history shows strong upward momentum).",
    "Volatility mismatch late horizon: the model’s late-horizon path is too smooth and too high; realized outcomes continue stepping down. Add a step-down regime adjustment: if last 10–15 days show lower lows, enforce continued lower lows in the forecast rather than flatlining."
  ]
}
